<1> Create new git repository from local.
- Create a directory to contain the project.
- Go into the new directory.
- In terminal: git init (git init -b <branch-name>, | --initial-branch=<branch-name>)

<2> Connect local repo to remote repo
- Create remote repository from github.com
- Add remote repository to local repository: 
git remote add <remote-name> <url>

- Add command on Windows platform:
git config core.autocrlf false
git config --global core.autocrlf false
(Prevent: LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it)

- Pull from remote to local before commit and push
 git pull <remote_name> <remote_branch_name>
 git pull origin main

<3> Push to remote:
git add .
git commit -m "message"
git push -u origin <branch>

- To push changes current tag to HEAD remote repository:
git push <remote-name> HEAD:refs/heads/<branch-name>

<4> git fetch
- Download commits, files, and refs from a remote repository into local repository.
git fetch origin
git fetch --all
git fetch origin master

- Displaying the commit logs in a compact, single-line format.
git log --oneline
git log --oneline origin/master

- Fetch all tags from the remote repository:
git fetch --tags:

<5> git merge
- Switch to the branch you want to merge into
git checkout <current-branch>

- Merge the changes from the <feature-branch> into the <current-branch>
git merge <feature-branch>

- Handling Merge Conflicts
# Resolve conflicts in the conflicted-file
# Add the resolved-file to the staging area
git add resolved-file

# Complete the merge by committing the changes
git commit

<5.1> Resolve conflic detail
- In <conflicted-file>:
<<<<<<< HEAD
Part_1 in current working tree which is difference from remote repository.
=======
Part_2 pull from remote repository which is difference from Part_1 in current working tree.
>>>>>>> 23563ef

-Resolve:
Accept Part_1 or Part_2 or both Part_1 and Path_2 for <conflicted-file>
git add <conflicted-file-resolved>
git commit -m "Resolve conflic complete in file <conflicted-file-resolved>"
git push

<6> git pull
git pull [<options>] [<repository> [<refspec>…​]]

# The git 'pull command' is used to fetch and integrate changes from a remote repository 
into the current branch. It combines the functionalities of 'git fetch' and 'git merge'.

- To pull changes from the default remote repository and merge them into the current branch:
git pull origin master

- Merge vs. Rebase: Using --rebase keeps your commit history linear, 
while a regular pull creates a merge commit.

- Force Pull: Use git pull --force with caution as it can overwrite local changes.

<7> git push
git push [<repository>] [<refspec>...]

- Sets the upstream tracking branch:
git push -u <remote-name> <branch-name>

- To push after setting the upstream branch:
git push

- To forcefully push changes, use the --force or -f flag:
git push <remote-name> --force  <branch-name>

- To delete a branch on the remote repository,  use the --delete option:
git push <remote-name> --delete  <branch-name>

- To push all branches to the remote repository, use the --all option:
git push <remote-name> --all

- To push a specific tag to the remote repository:
git push <remote-name> <tag-name>

- To push all tags to the remote repository, use the --tags option:
git push <remote-name> --tags

- To simulate a push without actually sending any data to the remote repository, 
use the --dry-run option:
git push <remote-name> --dry-run

<8> HEAD
# A reference to a commit object.
# You can think of the HEAD as the "current branch". When you switch branches with git checkout, 
the HEAD revision changes to point to the top of the new branch.

- You can see what HEAD points to by doing:
cat .git/HEAD

<9> git tag
- List the tags in the repository:
git tag
git tag -l

- To create a tag for the current commit:
git tag <tag-name>
Example:
git tag v1.0

- To create a tag for a specific commit:
git tag <tag-name> <commit-hash>

- To create an annotated tag, use the -a option and provide a message with the -m option:
git tag <tag_name> -a -m "message"

- To push a tag to a remote repository:
git push <remote-name> <tag-name>

- To push all tags to a remote repository:
git push <remote-name> --tags

- To delete a tag:
git tag --delete(-d) <tag-name>
git push --delete <remote-name> <tag-name>

- To rename a tag on remote repository:
git tag <new-tag-name> <old-tag-name> # Create a new tag with the new name.
git tag -d <old-tag-name> # Delete the old tag locally.
git push <remote-name> <new-tag-name> :<old-tag-name> # Push the new tag to the remote repository and delete the old tag.

<9> Retrieving the Current Commit Hash in Git
- To retrieve the current commit hash in Git, use the following command:
git rev-parse HEAD
git show -s --format=%H
git log -n 1 --format=format:"%H"

- Shortened Commit Hash:
git rev-parse --short HEAD

<10> git checkout
# Switch branches or restore working tree files.
- To switch to a branch:
git checkout <branch-name>

- To create a new branch and switch to it:
git checkout -b <branch-name>

- To restore a file to the state at a specific commit:
git checkout <commit-hash> -- <file-name>

- To restore all files to the state at a specific commit:
git checkout <commit-hash> -- .

- To restore a file to the state in the last commit:
git checkout -- <file-name>

- To restore all files to the state in the last commit:
git checkout -- .

- To restore a file to the state in the staging area:
git checkout -- <file-name>

- To restore all files to the state in the staging area:
git checkout -- .

- To restore a file to the state in the working directory:
git checkout HEAD -- <file-name>

- To restore all files to the state in the working directory:
git checkout HEAD -- .

- To restore a file to the state in the index:
git checkout -- <file-name>

- To restore all files to the state in the index:
git checkout -- .

- Detaching HEAD:
git checkout --detach HEAD~2

- Forcing a checkout: 
git checkout -f <branch-name>

- Merging changes:
git checkout -m <branch-name>

- Checking out a tag:
git checkout <tag-name>

- Creating a branch from a Tag:
git checkout -b <branch-name> <tag-name>

- Checking out a commit:
git checkout <commit-hash>

- Checking out a remote branch:
git checkout -b <branch-name> origin/<branch-name>

- Checkout the latest tag into a new branch:
git checkout -b <new-branch-name> $<latestTag>

- Working with Remote Tags:
git fetch --tags
git checkout <tag-name>
Then create a new branch from a remote tag:
git checkout -b <new-branch-name> <tag-name>

<11> git branch
# List, create, or delete branches.
- To creates a new branch pointing to the current commit:
git branch <branch-name>

- To create a new <remote-branch-name> and push current working directory to it:
git push <remote-name> HEAD:<remote-branch-name>

- To delete <remote-branch-name> from remote repository:
git push <remote-name> --delete <remote-branch-name>

- To create a new branch and switch to it immediately:
git switch -c <branch-name>
git checkout -b <branch-name>

- To delete a branch:
git branch -d <branch-name>

- To force delete a branch:
git branch -D <branch-name>

- To set the upstream branch for the current branch:
This sets the tracking information so that 'git pull' will appropriately merge from 
the upstream branch.
git branch --set-upstream-to=<upstream>

- To rename a branch:
If the new branch name already exists, use the -M option to force the rename.
git branch -m <old-branch-name> <new-branch-name>

- To list local branch:
git branch
git branch -l

- To list remote-tracking branches:
git branch -r

- To list all branches, including remote-tracking branches:
git branch -a

- To rename a remote Git Branch:
# Step 1: Delete the Old Remote Branch
git push <remote-name> --delete <old-branch-name>

# Step 2: Push the New Remote Branch
git push <remote-name> <new-branch-name>

<12> git log
# Displaying the commit logs in a compact, single-line format.
git log --oneline

- Log x HEAD on current branch:
git log --oneline -x

- Displaying all commits in the current branch:
git log --all

- Exit git log: press the q key.


<13> git remote 
- To list all the remotes associated with the repository:
git remote

- To list remotes with their URLs:
git remote -v

- To add a new remote repository:
git remote add <remote-name> <url>

Example:
git remote add origin https://github.com/user/repo.git

- To remove a remote:
git remote remove <remote-name>

Example:
git remote remove origin

- To rename a remote:
git remote rename <old-name> <new-name>
Example:
git remote rename origin upstream

- To fetch updates from a remote repository:
git fetch <remote-name>

- To change the URL of a remote repository:
git remote set-url <remote-name> <new-url>

- To set or delete the default branch for a remote:
git remote set-head <remote-name> (-a | --auto | -d | --delete | <branch>)

- To fetch updates for all remotes:
git remote update

- Displaying information about a remote:
git remote show <remote-name>
Example:
git remote show origin


<14> git submodule
- Clone repo with submodule:
git clone --recurse-submodules -j8 <url>

- Add submodule:
git submodule add <remote_url> <target_folder>

- Remove submodule:
git submodule deinit <submodule>
git rm <submodule>

- Shows the SHA-1 of the currently checked-out commit for each submodule, 
along with the submodule path:
git submodule status

- Pull a Git Submodule: (In parent local repository)
Allow git clone submodule to local repo, and auto checkout to HEAD:
git submodule update --init --recursive

Update Git submodule with latest commit.
git submodule update --remote --merge

- Push new commit to submodule:
cd <submodule-folder>
git checkout <branch-name>
git add .
git commit -m "New update to <submodule-folder>"
git push <remote-name> <branch-name>

<15> git reset
- Move the repository back to a previous commit, discarding any changes made after that commit:
(<commit-hash> being the first 7 characters of the commit hash we found in the log)
git log --oneline
git reset <commit-hash> 

- Undo changes in your working directory and get back to a specific commit while 
discarding all the commits made after that one:
git reset --hard <commit-hash>
Example:
git reset --hard 5f3a2e8 (discarding all the commits made after 5f3a2e8)
git reset --hard HEAD~1 (undo the last commit)

- Move HEAD to a previous commit HEAD~1, keeping lastest commit (HEAD) in staging area:
git reset --soft HEAD~1 

- Show only name of changed files:
git diff --name-only

- Show list of staged files:
git diff --name-only --cached

- Stage all changes:
git add .

- Unstage a file:
git reset <path--file-name> (GitCommand/GitCommand.xml)

- Unstage all changes for all files:
git reset


<16> git revert
- Revert the changes specified by the fourth last commit in HEAD and 
create a new commit with the reverted changes: 
git revert HEAD~3

- Exit the "reverting" state:
git revert --abort

- Continue the "reverting" process after resolving conflicts:
git revert --continue

<17> git restore
- Restore a file in the current directory to the state at the last commit:
git restore <path--file-name>

- Restore all files in the current directory to the state at the last commit:
git restore .

<18> git rm, git clean
- Remote a file in current directory:
git rm <path--file-name>

- See the files tracked on current branch:
git ls-tree -r master

- Remove untracked files:
git clean -f   

- Removing untracked directories:
git clean -f -d

<19> git merge:
- To merge <branch-a> to <branch-b>:
git checkout <branch-b>
git merge <branch-a>

<20> git rebase:
- Applying the changes from <branch-a> on top of <branch-b>:
git checkout <branch-b>
git rebase <branch-a>

<21> git switch
# Designed specifically to switch branches, making it clearer and safer than 'git checkout'.
- Switch to a branch:
git switch <branch>

- Creating and switching to a <new-branch-name> from last commit:
git switch -c <new-branch-name>

- Creating and switching to a <new-branch-name> from hash commit:
git switch -c <new-branch-name> hash

- Return HEAD to hash commit:
git switch --detach hash


<22> git diff
# Compare changes in code.
- Compare working directory and staging area:
git diff

- Compare staged changes with last commit:
git diff --staged

- Compare two branches:
git diff <branch-a> <branch-b>

- Compare two commits:
git diff <hash-commit-a> <hash-commit-b>

- Compare the changes in a specific file between the last commit and the working directory:
git diff HEAD <file-name>

<23> git commit --amend
- To amend the last commit with new changes:
git commit --amend

- To amend the last commit with a new message:
git commit --amend -m "New commit message"

- To amend the last commit without changing the commit message:
git commit --amend --no-edit

- To amend the last commit and add new changes without changing the commit message:
git add <file-name>
git commit --amend --no-edit

<24> git show-ref
# Displays references available in a local repository along with the associated commit IDs.
- To show the reference of the last commit:
git show-ref --head

- To show the reference of the last commit on a specific branch:
git show-ref --head <branch-name>

- To show the reference of the last commit on all branches:
git show-ref --head --dereference

 


